Then
\begin{align*}
|a \omega^2 + b + c \omega| &= a |e^{\pi i/3} \omega^2 + 1 + e^{\pi i} \omega| \\
&= \frac{a}{e^{\pi i/6}} |1 + e^{\pi i} \omega| \\
&= a |e^{5 \pi i/6} + \omega| \\
&= a \sqrt{\frac{3}{4} + \frac{1}{4} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\
&= \frac{a \sqrt{2 - \sqrt{3}}}{\sqrt{2}}.
\end{align*}Similarly,
\begin{align*}
|ax^2 + bx + c| &= a |x - \alpha| |x - \overline{\alpha}| \\
&= a |\omega^2 - \alpha| |\omega^2 - \overline{\alpha}| \\
&= a \cdot \frac{3}{2} (1 + \cos \theta),
\end{align*}where $\theta$ is the angle between $\omega^2$ and the real axis.

Let $\alpha = e^{i \theta},$ so $\cos \theta = \Re \alpha.$  Note that minimizing $1/2 + \sqrt{2 - \sqrt{3}}$ is equivalent to minimizing $3/2 (1 + \cos \theta).$

Using the unit circle, $\cos \theta = -\frac{1}{3}.$  Then
\[1 + \cos \theta = 1 - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3},\]so $\frac{a \sqrt{2 - \sqrt{3}}}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{a \sqrt{6 - 3 \sqrt{3}}}{2} = \frac{a \sqrt{3} \cdot \sqrt{2 - \sqrt{3}}}{2 \cdot \sqrt{3}} = \frac{a \sqrt{2 - \sqrt{3}}}{\sqrt{3}} = \frac{a}{\sqrt{3}} = 1.$  Hence, $a = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3},$ and the minimum value of $|a \omega^2 + b + c \omega|$ is $\boxed{1}.$ </think> <answer> 1 </answer>

